{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"associated prime associated prime","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md","fileName":"Associated Prime.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Associated Prime","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 20:05\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Associated Primes and Module Dimension]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Associated Primes and Module Dimension","Noetherian Ring","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"associated prime associated prime","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md","fileName":"Associated Prime.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Associated Prime","heading":"Associated Prime","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A prime ideal p is associated to an R-module M when p is the annihilator of some element of M. Equivalently, M contains a cyclic submodule isomorphic to R/p.\n\nThe set Ass(M) identifies primes directly visible through module elements. Over a [[Noetherian Ring]], a nonzero module has an associated prime, and a finitely generated module has only finitely many.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Associated Primes and Module Dimension","Noetherian Ring","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"associated prime associated prime","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Associated Prime.md","fileName":"Associated Prime.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Associated Prime